WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.060
Good to have you on SpaceX Daily, Episode 33.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:04.960
I'm Patrick and Vancouver.

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:07.800
Today is July 15th, 2026.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.140
Plenty moving across Starship, Falcon, and Starlink today.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:16.560
Starship Flight 13's planned debut of V3 satellites

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:20.220
will test whether the vehicle can deliver operational payloads

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.520
while advancing rapid reuse goals.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:27.520
Flight 13 will carry the first operational Starlink V3 satellites,

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:31.200
shifting the test campaign from pure vehicle validation

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.820
toward payload deployment.

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:35.640
The mission continues Starship's progression

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.020
toward orbital refueling demonstrations

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:40.500
required for higher energy trajectories.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.440
Earlier flights established the baseline

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:45.360
for booster and ship performance.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.140
This one adds real satellite hardware.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:50.980
Observers will track whether the new satellites

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:53.460
survive the higher energy-ascent profile.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.140
Starship last appeared in coverage

00:00:56.140 --> 00:00:58.880
on July 14th with the FAA clearance

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:00.680
for the next integrated test.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:03.180
This flight moves the open question

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:05.740
of payload integration one step forward.

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:08.320
Starbase teams are preparing ship

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:11.580
and booster hardware for the next integrated test flight.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:14.120
Ground systems and propellant loads

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.440
are being ready to head of the planned window.

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:19.240
The test follows completion of the prior

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:21.960
MISHAP investigation and incorporates lessons

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:23.240
on vehicle control.

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:28.000
Range and FAA final checks remain the immediate gating items.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:31.720
This continues the post investigation arc established

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:33.820
after the July 14 clearance.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:37.740
From the Falcon side, the Space Development Agency's

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:40.680
Tranch-1 Transport Layer E mission is scheduled

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:45.940
for a Falcon 9 liftoff from Vandenberg, SLC4E on July 16.

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:50.320
Booster B1103 will attempt its fourth flight

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:52.720
and land on OCISLY.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:57.080
The launch marks the 700 fifth SpaceX mission overall

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:58.780
and the 86th this year.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:01.840
Success requires precise orbital insertion

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:03.420
of the SDA spacecraft.

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:06.000
Watch for post-flight confirmation

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.780
of the 190th consecutive SpaceX launch success.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:14.960
The two-day 19-hour pad turnaround at SLC4E

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:17.600
continues the cadence tracked since the prior

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:18.700
Vandenberg flight.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:22.460
Our SpaceX users are tracking live updates

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:25.880
for the SDA Tranch-1 Transport Layer E mission,

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:29.900
including Booster B1103's fourth flight

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:32.220
and OCISLY landing.

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:36.000
Observers note the two-day 19-hour pad turnaround

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.400
at SLC4E as a data point on Vandenberg cadence.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.720
Discussion threads highlight the 45th launch

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:48.700
from the pad this year and the 212th OCISLY landing target.

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:52.080
Future Starship vehicles will no longer use

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:53.920
explosive termination systems

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:55.800
that destroy the vehicle on command.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:58.500
The change reflects the program shift

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.420
toward controlled flight termination

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:02.260
and eventual recovery hardware.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:05.320
Earlier test articles relied on the simpler

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:08.780
destructive method to stay within range safety envelopes.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:12.160
Removing the system reduces mass and complexity

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:14.560
while requiring new termination logic

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:17.240
that preserves vehicle integrity for reuse.

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:21.540
The update aligns with the ongoing arc toward booster catch

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:23.840
and ship return that has been central

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:25.600
since the last Starship segment.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:28.520
A Falcon 9 lifted off from Florida

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:31.000
and placed another batch of Starlink satellites

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:32.420
into their target orbits.

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:35.380
The mission used a flight proven first stage

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:38.080
continuing the booster reuse cadence

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:40.000
that has driven launch economics.

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:44.060
Payload deployment occurred after second stage separation

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:45.360
and faring jettison.

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:48.400
The flight adds to the constellation's growth

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:49.820
in low earth orbit.

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:52.940
Falcon 9 reuse counts have climbed steadily

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:55.580
since the 600th flight proven booster launch

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:56.620
covered yesterday.

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:00.760
A Falcon 9 is manifested to carry DARPA's satellite

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:03.340
servicing demonstration on July 21.

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:07.020
The mission will test on orbit repair techniques

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:09.520
using a dedicated servicing spacecraft.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:12.540
This adds a national security payload

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:16.660
to the Falcon manifest outside the Starlink and SDA cadence.

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:19.680
Range and vehicle availability will determine

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:20.860
whether the date holds.

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:23.980
The July 21 slot follows the pattern

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:27.240
of star shield adjacent contracts tracked in recent weeks.

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:31.020
Separate threads discuss the July 21 DARPA

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:33.680
satellite repair payload and its implications

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:36.360
for national security manifest diversity.

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:40.340
Regulators cleared the next Starship Integrated Flight Test

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:42.260
allowing the campaign to resume.

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:45.740
The approval follows review of prior flight data

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:47.360
and updated safety measures.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:50.740
Separately, Iranian statements have flagged

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:52.960
Starlink terminals as potential targets,

00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:55.800
raising questions about constellation resilience

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:57.060
in contested regions.

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:00.980
The dual developments highlight Starship's test cadence

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:03.720
alongside operational Starlink exposure.

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:07.520
Starlink's direct-to-sell and regional coverage questions

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:09.560
remain open from the prior episode.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:13.460
Jamaican and Cayman authorities issued temporary flight

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:16.380
restrictions for the expected Starship test window.

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:19.320
The notices cover potential overflight

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:20.480
and debris corridors.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:24.240
Similar restrictions have accompanied prior integrated flights

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:25.900
to protect civil aviation.

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:29.280
The measures activate only if the launch proceeds

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:30.600
on the notified day.

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:33.500
The restrictions follow the same pattern used

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:35.580
for the previous Starship test window.

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:41.060
Worth keeping an eye on, XAI installed 59 natural gas turbines

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:43.420
at the Colossus 2 site in Mississippi

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:46.280
without required federal clean air permits

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:49.100
according to regulatory filings and reporting.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:52.080
The installation has already released thousands

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:54.020
of tons of pollutants in an area

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:56.040
with elevated lung disease rates.

00:05:56.420 --> 00:05:58.400
Resolution would require the company

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:00.580
to secure permits retroactively

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:03.800
or modify operations to meet emission limits.

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:06.360
The episode follows earlier coverage

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:09.120
of Colossus power plant construction timelines.

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:13.720
On the AI front, cursor's critical arbitrary code execution

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:17.700
vulnerability reported seven months ago remains unfixed.

00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:20.480
Researchers have now published full details

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:24.200
that allow executions simply by opening a poisoned repository.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:26.880
The issue affects millions of users

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:28.580
of the AI code editor.

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:32.340
The flaw was first disclosed in December 2025

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:34.140
and has drawn renewed attention

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:37.480
after full exploit details appeared on public forums.

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:40.640
Claude's core designer has joined cursor,

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:43.360
strengthening the team behind the AI editor

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:46.220
that distributes grok and other models to developers.

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:49.760
The higher ads expertise in model integration

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:52.360
and agent tooling at a moment when cursor

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:55.640
is expanding its forward deployed engineering offerings.

00:06:56.600 --> 00:07:00.600
Elon Musk acquired a $1 billion gas turbine company

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:04.100
to supply power for XAI's grok training clusters.

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:07.720
The purchase provides dedicated generation capacity

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:10.400
for the expanding Colossus II installation

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:14.280
and follows the earlier installation of 59 turbines

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:15.680
at the Mississippi site.

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:17.680
From an engineering standpoint,

00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:20.760
Starship Flight 13 will attempt to place

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:23.860
the first V3 Starlink satellites into orbit,

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:26.680
moving the vehicle from pure test article

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:27.900
to payload carrier.

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:30.880
The satellites are heavier and more capable

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:32.220
than prior generations,

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:36.420
requiring the ship to deliver precise orbital insertion energy

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:38.660
while preserving enough propellant margin

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:40.140
for a controlled reentry.

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:44.140
From first principles, every kilogram of satellite mass

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:47.200
reduces the propellant available for the return burn.

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:51.180
The V3 design therefore trades added capability

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:54.440
against tighter margins on the ship's Delta V budget.

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:58.760
The test also exercises the new payload deployment hardware

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:01.340
under flight-like vibration and thermal loads

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:03.760
that ground tests cannot fully replicate.

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:07.260
Success would validate that the current Starship stack

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:09.100
can handle operational traffic

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:11.720
without dedicated upper-stage modifications.

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:15.340
The immediate watch item is whether the satellites

00:08:15.340 --> 00:08:18.300
separate cleanly and acquire their initial attitude

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:20.940
before the ship performs its deorbit maneuver.

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:24.800
The decision to retire explosive flight termination systems

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:27.560
on future Starships follows the same logic.

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:31.400
Destructive charges add mass and introduce failure modes

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:34.580
that become unacceptable once the vehicle must survive

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:35.260
to landing.

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:39.080
Removing them forces the guidance and control software

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:41.660
to achieve termination through engine cutoff

00:08:41.660 --> 00:08:43.220
and attitude commands alone.

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:46.460
That change lowers the vehicle's dry mass

00:08:46.460 --> 00:08:48.880
and simplifies the recovery architecture,

00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:52.520
directly improving the economics of rapid reuse.

00:08:53.220 --> 00:08:56.440
It also tightens the requirement on range safety modeling

00:08:56.440 --> 00:08:59.800
because the vehicle remains intact longer during an abort.

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:03.680
The net effect is a higher fidelity test of the systems

00:09:03.680 --> 00:09:07.020
that will eventually support booster catch and ship return.

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:11.040
The Falcon 9's continued use of flight proven boosters

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:14.940
on Starlink missions illustrates a parallel cost reduction path.

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:19.740
Each reuse cycle amortizes the original manufacturing cost

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:24.040
across more flights, shrinking the effective per kilogram expense

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:26.780
toward the raw material floor of the vehicle.

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:31.080
The 600th flight proven booster launch already demonstrated

00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:34.080
that the structural and thermal margins support dozens

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:34.780
of cycles.

00:09:35.500 --> 00:09:39.540
Before we go, watch for the July 21 DARPA servicing demo

00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:41.820
and the Starship Flight 13 window

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:43.940
that follows the new airspace notices.

